+Chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting reduces arithmetic answers that follow misleading
+variable names rather than the equations. Building on \citeauthor{kudo2026faithful}'s
+arithmetic study, we construct matched problems in which a variable's name denotes an
+incorrect digit while the equations remain unchanged. Across \NUM{n-models} models and
+three sets of worked examples, we compare final-answer-only prompts with CoT prompts
+that request the calculation before the answer. Under final-answer-only prompting,
+misleading names increase answers matching the named digit. Under CoT, the difference
+from ordinary names meets a $\pm\NUM{eq-delta}$-percentage-point equivalence bound in
+\NUM{eq-cot-equivalent} of \NUM{eq-cot-cells} comparisons. The matched design isolates
+resistance to irrelevant words as a measurable benefit of written arithmetic reasoning:
+asking for the calculation reduces the influence of a competing answer suggested by the name.
 \end{abstract}
 
